    "content": "Former British Prime Minister Boris Johnson has condemned a Russian drone attack on a passenger train traveling near the Polish border in Ukraine's Volyn region. The incident occurred on September 13, 2026, when an unmanned aerial vehicle struck a train on the Kyiv-Warsaw route. Ukraine's state railway company, Ukrzaliznytsia, reported that the attack caused no deaths or injuries.\n\nJohnson, who served as UK prime minister from 2019 to 2022, has been a vocal supporter of Ukraine since Russia's full-scale invasion in February 2022. His condemnation adds to a growing chorus of international criticism over Russia's continued use of drone strikes against civilian infrastructure. The Volyn region, located in western Ukraine, shares a border with Poland, a NATO member state, making the attack particularly sensitive given its proximity to alliance territory.\n\nUkrzaliznytsia, which operates the country's extensive rail network, has kept trains running throughout the war despite repeated attacks. The Kyiv-Warsaw route is a vital link for refugees, humanitarian aid, and diplomatic travel. The railway has become a symbol of Ukrainian resilience, with staff often risking their lives to maintain service.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this event underscores the broader instability affecting global travel and security. Many Somalis in Europe rely on rail connections for family visits, business, and resettlement, and disruptions in Eastern Europe can ripple through wider transportation networks. Additionally, the attack highlights the dangers faced by civilians in conflict zones, a reality familiar to many Somalis who have experienced war and displacement.\n\nThe strike also raises questions about the safety of international transport corridors and the potential for escalation given the proximity to NATO territory. While no group has claimed responsibility, the attack is consistent with Russia's pattern of targeting Ukrainian infrastructure. International observers continue to monitor the situation, with calls for de-escalation and accountability.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/09/13/ruushka-oo-tareen-rakaab-ku-weeraray-xadka-poland/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=ruushka-oo-tareen-rakaab-ku-weeraray-xadka-poland",
