    "id": "news-1789321683400-5",
    "title": "Former Somali Minister Warns Mogadishu at Risk if AU Troops Withdraw",
    "original_title": "Somalia: Mogadishu could fall without AU troops, says ex-minister",
    "slug": "former-somali-minister-warns-mogadishu-at-risk-if-au-troops-withdraw",
    "summary": "Abdirahman Aynte, ex-Minister of Planning, says Mogadishu cannot be defended without African Union forces, as the US plans to end logistics funding for the peacekeeping mission.",
    "content": "MOGADISHU, Somalia — Abdirahman Aynte, Somalia's former Minister of Planning, has warned that Mogadishu cannot be defended if African Union troops withdraw from the country by December. In a statement reported by Somalia Guardian, Aynte also said that US diplomats have informed Somali officials that Washington will not reverse its decision to end logistics funding for the AU peacekeeping mission, regardless of the outcome of ongoing political talks.\n\nThe warning follows the United States' formal notification to the African Union on July 1 that it would stop funding the UN Support Office in Somalia (UNSOS). UNSOS provides logistical support to the AU Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS), including food, fuel, and medical supplies. The US has been a major financial contributor to the mission, and its withdrawal of support could severely hamper operations.\n\nThis development comes amid a planned drawdown of AU forces, with the mission expected to fully exit by December 2024. The Somali government has been preparing to take over security responsibilities, but concerns remain about the capability of Somali national forces to counter al-Shabaab militants, who have intensified attacks in recent months. The international community has repeatedly stressed the need for a conditions-based withdrawal to avoid security vacuums.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the potential fall of Mogadishu would be a devastating blow. Many diaspora members have invested in rebuilding the city, and a security collapse could reverse years of progress. It would also exacerbate the humanitarian crisis, potentially triggering a new wave of displacement and refugees. Diaspora remittances, which are a lifeline for many families, could be disrupted if the situation deteriorates.\n\nAynte's comments underscore the fragility of the security situation and the importance of sustained international support. The Somali government has yet to comment on the US decision, but officials have previously called for a slower transition to prevent a security vacuum. The African Union and United Nations are expected to discuss the funding gap in upcoming meetings.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-mogadishu-fall-risk-aynte-aussom-withdrawal/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
    "source_name": "Somalia Guardian",
    "original_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-mogadishu-fall-risk-aynte-aussom-withdrawal/",
    "country": "Somalia",
    "category": "Security",
    "published_at": "2026-09-13T16:11:48.000Z",
    "first_seen_at": "2026-09-13T17:48:03.400Z",
